 3 4 5 6 7 8 9 10\n4 602436426\n2 6 7 9\n6 24979445\n2 3 4 5 8 10\n4 861648772\n2 4 8 9\n\nSample Output 3\n\n1202115217",
    "prediction": "openai/gpt-4o",
    "generated_result": {
      "generated_answer": "To solve this problem, we will implement a function that uses the Kruskal's algorithm to determine whether the graph is connected after all edge addition operations and, if so, computes the total weight of the edges in a minimum spanning tree (MST) of the graph. This algorithm efficiently finds the MST by utilizing a union-find (disjoint set) data structure to manage the connected components. Here is how you can implement it:\n\n```python\ndef solve():\n    import sys\n    input = sys.stdin.read\n    data = input().split()\n\n    # Read N and M\n    index = 0\n    N = int(data[index])\n    index += 1\n    M = int(data[index])\n    index += 1\n\n    edges = []\n\n    for _ in range(M):\n        # Read number of vertices in the subset and the weight for this operation\n        K = int(data[index])\n        index += 1\n        C = int(data[index])\n        index += 1\n\n        # Read the actual vertices in the subset\n        vertices = list(map(int, data[index:index + K]))\n        index += K\n\n        # Add edge for each pair of vertices (u < v)\n        for i in range(K):\n            for j in range(i + 1, K):\n                u, v = vertices[i], vertices[j]\n                edges.append((C, u, v))\n\n    # Sort edges by weight\n    edges.sort()\n\n    # Union-Find data structure to manage connected components\n    parent = list(range(N + 1))\n    rank = [0] * (N + 1)\n\n    def find(x):\n        if parent[x] != x:\n            parent[x] = find(parent[x])\n        return parent[x]\n\n    def union(x, y):\n        rootX = find(x)\n        rootY = find(y)\n        if rootX != rootY:\n            if rank[rootX] > rank[rootY]:\n                parent[rootY] = rootX\n            elif rank[rootX] < rank[rootY]:\n                parent[rootX] = rootY\n            else:\n                parent[rootY] = rootX\n                rank[rootX] += 1\n            return True\n        return False\n\n    mst_weight = 0\n    num_edges_used = 0\n\n    # Implementing Kruskal's algorithm\n    for weight, u, v in edges:\n        if union(u, v):\n            mst_weight += weight\n            num_edges_used += 1\n            if num_edges_used == N - 1:\n                break\n\n    # Check if all N-1 edges are used in MST (means graph is connected)\n    if num_edges_used == N - 1:\n        print(mst_weight)\n    else:\n        print(-1)\n\n# To execute the function when the script is called\nsolve()\n```\n\n### Instructions for Use:\n- This function reads from standard input, so to use it, you can redirect an input file or pipe input directly into the script.\n- It processes this input to simulate the operations described, builds the graph, and performs the operations to check connectivity and compute the MST weight. If the graph is not connected after processing all operations, it outputs `-1`.\n- Make sure the `solve()` function is called, as done at the end of the script, to execute the solution logic.",
