      "generated_answer": "Looking at this problem, I need to find the sum of power for all non-empty groups of heroes, where the power of a group is defined as (max of group)^2 * (min of group).\n\nThe key insight is that instead of enumerating all possible groups (which would be 2^n), I can use a mathematical approach by considering each element's contribution as the maximum or minimum in various groups.\n\nLet me think about this systematically:\n\n1. If I sort the array, then for any subsequence, the min and max will be at specific positions in the sorted array.\n\n2. For each element at position i in the sorted array, I can calculate how many groups have this element as the maximum and what the minimum would be in those groups.\n\n3. When element at position i is the maximum, the minimum can be any element from position 0 to i. For a group where nums[j] is the minimum and nums[i] is the maximum (with j ≤ i), the number of such groups is 2^(i-j-1) (choosing any subset of elements between j and i).\n\n4. Special case: when j = i (single element group), there are no elements in between, so we have 1 group.\n\nActually, let me reconsider with a cleaner approach:\n- Sort the array\n- For each position i, calculate the contribution when nums[i] is the maximum\n- When nums[i] is the maximum, we can choose any subset of elements from indices 0 to i-1 to include, and we must include at least one element as the minimum\n- The sum of all possible minimums when nums[i] is maximum can be calculated efficiently\n\nWait, let me think again. A better approach:\n- Sort the array\n- For each i, when nums[i] is the maximum, we can include any subset of elements from indices 0 to i-1\n- The minimum of the group will be the smallest element we choose from indices 0 to i\n- If we include nums[i] alone, min = nums[i]\n- If we include nums[i] and some other elements, min = min of the chosen elements from 0 to i-1\n\nActually, let me use this approach:\nFor each i from 0 to n-1, calculate the contribution when nums[i] is the maximum:\n\n- Contribution when nums[i] is maximum = nums[i]^2 × (sum of minimums of all non-empty subsets of nums[0...i])\n\nFor the subarray nums[0...i], the sum of minimums of all non-empty subsets can be calculated as:\nsum[j=0 to i] nums[j] × 2^(i-j)\n\nThis is because for each nums[j], it contributes to the minimum in all subsets that contain nums[j] but no elements smaller than nums[j] from indices j+1 to i. Since the array is sorted, nums[j] is the minimum in any subset that contains nums[j] and any subset of elements from indices j+1 to i, which gives 2^(i-j) subsets.\n\nBut actually, for a sorted array, nums[j] is the minimum in exactly 2^(i-j) subsets of nums[0...i] that contain nums[j] (we can choose any subset of the i-j elements after j).\n\n```python\ndef sumOfPower(nums):\n    MOD = 10**9 + 7\n    nums.sort()\n    n = len(nums)\n    result = 0\n    \n    # prefix_sum will store the sum of min contributions for subsets ending at current position\n    # Specifically, prefix_sum = sum of (nums[j] * 2^(i-j)) for j=0 to i\n    prefix_sum = 0\n    \n    for i in range(n):\n        # When nums[i] is the maximum, the sum of minimums of all non-empty subsets \n        # of nums[0...i] is prefix_sum + nums[i]\n        # prefix_sum contains sum of nums[j] * 2^(i-1-j) * 2 = 2 * previous prefix_sum\n        # But we need to recalculate\n        \n        # Actually, let's maintain the sum S where S = sum of nums[j] * 2^(i-j) for j=0 to i\n        # For new i: S_new = nums[i] + 2 * S_old\n        prefix_sum = (nums[i] + 2 * prefix_sum) % MOD\n        \n        # Contribution when nums[i] is maximum: nums[i]^2 * (sum of minimums of non-empty subsets of nums[0...i])\n        contribution = ((nums[i] * nums[i]) % MOD) * prefix_sum\n        result = (result + contribution) % MOD\n    \n    return result\n```",
