      "generated_answer": "Here is the Python function that implements the solution to the given problem:\n\n```python\ndef sum_of_powers(nums):\n    MOD = 10**9 + 7\n    n = len(nums)\n    nums.sort()\n    \n    max_contributions = [0] * n\n    min_contributions = [0] * n\n    \n    # Calculate max contributions using 2^i as there are 2^i subsets where nums[i] is the maximum\n    power_of_2 = 1\n    for i in range(n):\n        max_contributions[i] = nums[i] * power_of_2 % MOD\n        power_of_2 = (power_of_2 * 2) % MOD\n    \n    # Calculate min contributions using 2^(n-1-i) as there are 2^(n-1-i) subsets where nums[i] is the minimum\n    power_of_2 = 1\n    for i in range(n-1, -1, -1):\n        min_contributions[i] = nums[i] * power_of_2 % MOD\n        power_of_2 = (power_of_2 * 2) % MOD\n    \n    # Sum up all contributions\n    total_power = 0\n    for i in range(n):\n        total_power = (total_power + max_contributions[i] * min_contributions[i]) % MOD\n    \n    return total_power\n```\n\nThis function utilizes sorting and computes contributions from both ends of the sorted array. For each element considered as the maximum, we calculate its contribution using powers of two indicative of the subsets it could be a maximum for. Similarly, for minimum contributions, powers of two are used from the other end. Finally, the function calculates the sum of these contributions while taking care to apply the modulo operator at each step to prevent overflow and ensure the result fits within the constraints.",
