    "global index": "LiveCodeBench_6",
    "prompt": "Generate an executable Python function generated from the given prompt. The function should take stdin as input and print the output. Simply call the function after the definition. Question:\nAlex is participating in the filming of another video of BrMeast, and BrMeast asked Alex to prepare 250 thousand tons of TNT, but Alex didn't hear him well, so he prepared $n$ boxes and arranged them in a row waiting for trucks. The $i$-th box from the left weighs $a_i$ tons.\n\nAll trucks that Alex is going to use hold the same number of boxes, denoted by $k$. Loading happens the following way:\n\n \n-  The first $k$ boxes goes to the first truck, \n-  The second $k$ boxes goes to the second truck, \n-  $\\dotsb$ \n-  The last $k$ boxes goes to the $\\frac{{n}}{{k}}$-th truck. Upon loading is completed, each truck must have exactly $k$ boxes. In other words, if at some point it is not possible to load exactly $k$ boxes into the truck, then the loading option with that $k$ is not possible.\n\nAlex hates justice, so he wants the maximum absolute difference between the total weights of two trucks to be as great as possible. If there is only one truck, this value is $0$.\n\nAlex has quite a lot of connections, so for every $1 \\leq k \\leq n$, he can find a company such that each of its trucks can hold exactly $k$ boxes. Print the maximum absolute difference between the total weights of any two trucks.\n\nInput\n\nThe first line contains one integer $t$ ($1 \\leq t \\leq 10^4$) — the number of test cases.\n\nThe first line of each test case contains one integer $n$ ($1 \\leq n \\leq 150\\,000$) — the number of boxes.\n\nThe second line contains $n$ integers $a_1, a_2, \\dots, a_n$ ($1 \\leq a_i \\leq 10^9$) — the weights of the boxes.\n\nIt is guaranteed that the sum of $n$ for all test cases does not exceed $150\\,000$.\n\nOutput\n\nFor each test case, print a single integer — the answer to the problem.Sample Input 1:\n5\n\n2\n\n1 2\n\n6\n\n10 2 3 6 1 3\n\n4\n\n1000000000 1000000000 1000000000 1000000000\n\n15\n\n60978 82265 78961 56708 39846 31071 4913 4769 29092 91348 64119 72421 98405 222 14294\n\n8\n\n19957 69913 37531 96991 57838 21008 14207 19198\n\n\n\nSample Output 1:\n\n1\n9\n0\n189114\n112141\n\n\nNote\n\nIn the first case, we should pick two trucks, so the first one will have only the first box, and the second one will have only the second box.\n\nIn the second case, we should pick six trucks, so the maximum will be $10$, the minimum will be $1$, and the answer is $10 - 1 = 9$.\n\nIn the third case, for any possible $k$, the trucks will have the same total weight of boxes, so the answer is $0$.",
    "prediction": "deepseek/deepseek-v3.2",
    "generated_result": {
      "generated_answer": "We need to solve this step-by-step.\n\nThe problem is:  \nFor each \\( k \\) from \\( 1 \\) to \\( n \\) (where \\( k \\) divides \\( n \\) evenly),  \nwe split the array into \\( \\frac{n}{k} \\) trucks, each with \\( k \\) consecutive boxes.  \nWe want the *maximum absolute difference between total weights of any two trucks* to be as large as possible,  \nand we can choose any \\( k \\) that works (i.e., \\( n \\) divisible by \\( k \\)).\n\nFrom the examples, it looks like we are asked to compute, for each test case,  \nthe maximum possible difference across all valid \\( k \\).\n\n---\n\n## Step-by-step reasoning\n\n### 1. Understanding the grouping\nIf \\( k \\) divides \\( n \\), we have \\( m = n/k \\) trucks.\n\nTruck \\( j \\) (1-based) gets boxes from indices \\( (j-1)k \\) to \\( jk - 1 \\) (inclusive).\n\nSo truck weights are:\n\n\\[\nS_j = \\sum_{i=(j-1)k}^{jk-1} a_i\n\\]\n\nWe want the difference between the max \\( S_j \\) and min \\( S_j \\) over all \\( j \\) for that \\( k \\).\n\nThen, over all \\( k \\), we want the maximum of that difference.\n\n---\n\n### 2. Observations\nIf \\( k = n \\), then only 1 truck, difference = 0.\n\nIf \\( k = 1 \\), then each truck is one box, difference = max(\\( a_i \\)) - min(\\( a_i \\)).\n\nBut the maximum difference might occur for some intermediate \\( k \\), because grouping can make some trucks much heavier and others much lighter.\n\n---\n\n### 3. Efficient computation\nWe need to check all divisors \\( k \\) of \\( n \\) (since only those are valid).\n\nFor each \\( k \\), we compute sums of consecutive blocks of length \\( k \\) and find max/min.\n\nDirect computation for all \\( k \\) would be:\nFor each \\( k \\), we have \\( n/k \\) sums, each sum takes \\( O(k) \\) to compute naive, total \\( O(n) \\) per \\( k \\).  \nBut if we do for all divisors of \\( n \\), total complexity could be large if we do naive sum each time.\n\nBetter:  \nWe can precompute prefix sums, then each truck sum = prefix[j*k] - prefix[(j-1)*k],  \nso each \\( k \\) evaluation is \\( O(n/k) \\) time.\n\nSum over all divisors \\( k \\) of \\( n \\):  \n\\[\n\\sum_{k \\mid n} \\frac{n}{k} = n \\sum_{k \\mid n} \\frac{1}{k} \\leq n \\cdot H(n)\n\\]\nwhere \\( H(n) \\) is sum of reciprocals of divisors, which is \\( O(\\log n) \\) average.  \nSo total per test case ~ \\( O(n \\log n) \\).\n\n---\n\n### 4. Implementation plan\nFor each test case:\n- Find all divisors of \\( n \\) (from 1 to n where n % k == 0).\n- Compute prefix sums.\n- For each divisor \\( k \\):\n  - Compute truck sums using prefix sums.\n  - Find max and min sum.\n  - Compute difference.\n- Keep global maximum difference over all \\( k \\).\n- Output it.\n\n---\n\n### 5. Writing the code\n\n```python\nimport sys\n\ndef solve():\n    input_data = sys.stdin.read().strip().split()\n    t = int(input_data[0])\n    idx = 1\n    out_lines = []\n    \n    for _ in range(t):\n        n = int(input_data[idx]); idx += 1\n        a = list(map(int, input_data[idx:idx + n])); idx += n\n        \n        # Prefix sums\n        prefix = [0] * (n + 1)\n        for i in range(n):\n            prefix[i + 1] = prefix[i] + a[i]\n        \n        # Find divisors of n\n        divisors = []\n        for k in range(1, int(n**0.5) + 1):\n            if n % k == 0:\n                divisors.append(k)\n                if k != n // k:\n                    divisors.append(n // k)\n        \n        best_diff = 0\n        \n        for k in divisors:\n            m = n // k\n            truck_sums = []\n            for j in range(m):\n                start = j * k\n                end = (j + 1) * k\n                truck_sum = prefix[end] - prefix[start]\n                truck_sums.append(truck_sum)\n            max_sum = max(truck_sums)\n            min_sum = min(truck_sums)\n            diff = max_sum - min_sum\n            if diff > best_diff:\n                best_diff = diff\n        \n        out_lines.append(str(best_diff))\n    \n    sys.stdout.write(\"\\n\".join(out_lines))\n\nif __name__ == \"__main__\":\n    solve()\n```",
