ee fruits from `j+1` to `j + (j+1)` inclusive? Wait, compute:  \nj=0 → j+1 = 1 to j + (j+1) = 0+1 = 1, so just index 1. Yes.  \nj=1 → j+1=2 to j+(j+1)=1+2=3, indices 2,3. Yes.  \n\nSo free range in 0-index: start = j+1, end = j + (j+1) = 2j+1 inclusive.  \n\nThus, in DP:  \nIf we buy at `j`, we skip to index `2j+2` next (since free up to 2j+1, next to consider is 2j+2).  \n\nSo transition:  \n`dp[i] = min( dp[i+1] + prices[i], dp[2*i+3] + prices[i] )`? Wait, careful:  \n\nWe define `dp[pos]` = min coins to buy fruits from `pos` to end (pos 0-index).  \nAt `pos`, we can either take it free (if covered by previous purchase) — but that’s handled by previous states jumping over it, or buy it.  \n\nActually simpler: We iterate from end to start, and at each `i`, we try buying it:  \nCost = prices[i] + dp[min(n, 2*i+2)] because after buying i, next unpaid fruit is at index 2*i+2.  \n\nBut we must also consider case where we don’t buy it — but if we don’t buy, we must have been given free by earlier purchase. So we can’t just skip without buying unless covered.  \n\nBetter approach:  \nLet `dp[i]` = min coins to get first i fruits (1-indexed). Then for i-th fruit, either:  \n- Get it free (if covered by earlier purchase)  \n- Buy it: cost = dp[i-1] + prices[i-1], and it covers up to i + i fruits.  \n\nBut tracking coverage is messy.  \n\nInstead, think greedy? Not obvious.  \n\nGiven constraints n ≤ 1000, O(n²) DP is fine:  \n\nDefine `dp[i]` = min coins to get fruits 1..i (1-indexed).  \nInitialize dp[0] = 0.  \nFor i = 1..n:  \nWe can get fruit i by either:  \n1. Buying some earlier fruit j (1 ≤ j ≤ i) that covers i (i ≤ j + j), then fruit i is free. But we still need to pay for fruit j and others before.  \nBetter: At fruit i, we choose to buy it: then it covers up to i+i. So dp[min(n, i+i)] = min(itself, dp[i-1] + prices[i-1]).  \nWait, but buying i affects future, not past.  \n\nActually, standard approach for this “buy i, get next i free” problem:  \nWe do DP from end: dp[i] = min cost for fruits i..n.  \nAt i:  \n- Option 1: Buy i: cost = prices[i-1] + dp[min(n+1, i+i+1)]? Wait, i is 1-index: buying i covers i+1 .. i+i. So next uncovered is i+i+1.  \nSo dp[i] = prices[i-1] + dp[min(n+1, i+i+1)].  \n- Option 2: Get i for free: then dp[i] = dp[i+1].  \nBut i can be free only if some earlier purchase covers it. But in DP from end, “earlier” means smaller index, so we can’t know. Hmm.  \n\nBetter: Let’s do forward DP:  \ndp[i] = min cost for first i fruits. Initially dp[0]=0.  \nFor i from 1 to n:  \nWe can get i by either:  \n1. Buy i: then dp[i] = dp[i-1] + prices[i-1].  \n2. Get i for free: then some j < i was bought with j >= i - j? Wait, free coverage: if j bought, covers j+1 .. j+j. So i is free if j < i ≤ j+j.  \nSo for current i, we try all j from 1 to i-1, if i ≤ 2j, then i could be free from j, so dp[i] = min(dp[i], dp[j-1] + prices[j-1])? Wait, careful:  \nIf we buy j to cover i, then all fruits from j to i are free, so cost = dp[j-1] + prices[j-1].  \n\nThus:  \ndp[i] = min( dp[i-1] + prices[i-1], min over j where 2*j >= i of dp[j-1] + prices[j-1] ).  \n\nBut this is O(n²), fine for n=1000.  \n\nWe can implement it.  \n\nHere’s the Python function:  \n\n```python\ndef minimumCoins(prices):\n    n = len(prices)\n    dp = [float('inf')] * (n + 1)\n    dp[0] = 0\n    for i in range(1, n + 1):\n        # Option 1: buy i\n        dp[i] = min(dp[i], dp[i - 1] + prices[i - 1])\n        # Option 2: i is free from some earlier purchase j\n        for j in range(1, i):\n            if 2 * j >= i:\n                dp[i] = min(dp[i], dp[j - 1] + prices[j - 1])\n    return dp[n]\n```",
