    "generated_result": {
      "generated_answer": "Looking at this problem, I need to find the number of ways to express `n` as a sum of unique positive integers raised to the power `x`.\n\nThis is a classic dynamic programming problem similar to the subset sum problem, but with the constraint that we use unique integers and their x-th powers.\n\nApproach:\n1. Generate all possible x-th powers of positive integers that are ≤ n\n2. Use dynamic programming where dp[i] represents the number of ways to make sum i\n3. For each possible x-th power, update the dp array in reverse order to avoid counting the same number multiple times\n\n```python\ndef numberOfWays(n, x):\n    MOD = 10**9 + 7\n    \n    # Generate all x-th powers of positive integers that are <= n\n    powers = []\n    i = 1\n    while i**x <= n:\n        powers.append(i**x)\n        i += 1\n    \n    # dp[i] represents the number of ways to make sum i\n    dp = [0] * (n + 1)\n    dp[0] = 1  # One way to make sum 0: use no numbers\n    \n    # For each x-th power, update the dp array\n    for power in powers:\n        # Traverse backwards to avoid using the same number multiple times\n        for j in range(n, power - 1, -1):\n            dp[j] = (dp[j] + dp[j - power]) % MOD\n    \n    return dp[n]\n```",
