      "generated_answer": "We can solve this by using a frequency map and dynamic programming with a sliding window to handle large counts efficiently.  \n\nHere’s the Python function:\n\n```python\ndef countSubMultisets(nums, l, r):\n    MOD = 10**9 + 7\n    from collections import Counter\n    \n    freq = Counter(nums)\n    \n    total = sum(nums)\n    if l > total:\n        return 0\n    \n    r = min(r, total)\n    \n    # dp[x] = number of sub-multisets with sum exactly x\n    dp = [0] * (r + 1)\n    dp[0] = 1  # empty multiset\n    \n    # Special handling for 0s\n    zero_count = freq[0]\n    # Multiply dp[0] by (zero_count + 1) because 0 can be chosen 0..zero_count times in any subset\n    dp[0] = zero_count + 1\n    \n    for val, cnt in freq.items():\n        if val == 0:\n            continue\n        # prefix sum array for sliding window\n        prefix = [0] * (r + 1)\n        for sum_val in range(r + 1):\n            # prefix[sum_val] = dp[sum_val] + dp[sum_val - val] + ... up to cnt terms\n            # We can compute with formula:\n            # prefix[sum_val] = dp[sum_val] + (sum_val >= val ? prefix[sum_val - val] : 0)\n            # but careful with counting only up to cnt terms.\n            # Better to use standard bounded knapsack with prefix sums:\n            # Let pref[sum] = dp[sum] + pref[sum - val] - (sum >= (cnt+1)*val ? dp[sum - (cnt+1)*val] : 0)\n            # Actually simpler: for each sum_val, we want sum_{k=0..cnt} dp[sum_val - k*val]\n            # We'll compute prefix sums of dp, then use subtraction to get range sums.\n            pass  # placeholder for logic\n        \n        # Efficient way: use prefix sums of dp for sliding window\n        new_dp = dp[:]\n        for rem in range(val):\n            # process indices rem, rem+val, rem+2*val, ...\n            s = 0\n            queue = []\n            for idx in range(rem, r + 1, val):\n                queue.append(dp[idx])\n                s += dp[idx]\n                if len(queue) > cnt + 1:\n                    s -= queue.pop(0)\n                new_dp[idx] = s % MOD\n        dp = new_dp\n    \n    # Sum dp[l..r]\n    result = 0\n    for s in range(l, r + 1):\n        result = (result + dp[s]) % MOD\n    \n    return result\n```",
