    "global index": "LiveCodeBench_277",
    "prompt": "Generate an executable Python function generated from the given prompt. The function should take stdin as input and print the output. Simply call the function after the definition. Question:\nTakahashi is displaying a sentence with N words in a window.\r\nAll words have the same height, and the width of the i-th word (1\\leq i\\leq N) is L _ i.\nThe words are displayed in the window separated by a space of width 1.\r\nMore precisely, when the sentence is displayed in a window of width W, the following conditions are satisfied.\n\n- The sentence is divided into several lines.\n- The first word is displayed at the beginning of the top line.\n- The i-th word (2\\leq i\\leq N) is displayed either with a gap of 1 after the (i-1)-th word, or at the beginning of the line below the line containing the (i-1)-th word. It will not be displayed anywhere else.\n- The width of each line does not exceed W. Here, the width of a line refers to the distance from the left end of the leftmost word to the right end of the rightmost word.\n\nWhen Takahashi displayed the sentence in the window, the sentence fit into M or fewer lines.\r\nFind the minimum possible width of the window.\n\nInput\n\nThe input is given from Standard Input in the following format:\nN M\r\nL _ 1 L _ 2 \\ldots L _ N\n\nOutput\n\nPrint the answer in one line.\n\nConstraints\n\n\n- 1\\leq M\\leq N\\leq2\\times10 ^ 5\n- 1\\leq L _ i\\leq10^9\\ (1\\leq i\\leq N)\n- All input values are integers.\n\nSample Input 1\n\n13 3\r\n9 5 2 7 1 8 8 2 1 5 2 3 6\n\nSample Output 1\n\n26\r\n\nWhen the width of the window is 26, you can fit the given sentence into three lines as follows.\n\nYou cannot fit the given sentence into three lines when the width of the window is 25 or less, so print 26.\nNote that you should not display a word across multiple lines, let the width of a line exceed the width of the window, or rearrange the words.\n\nSample Input 2\n\n10 1\r\n1000000000 1000000000 1000000000 1000000000 1000000000 1000000000 1000000000 1000000000 1000000000 1000000000\n\nSample Output 2\n\n10000000009\r\n\nNote that the answer may not fit into a 32\\operatorname{{bit}} integer.\n\nSample Input 3\n\n30 8\r\n8 55 26 97 48 37 47 35 55 5 17 62 2 60 23 99 73 34 75 7 46 82 84 29 41 32 31 52 32 60\n\nSample Output 3\n\n189",
    "prediction": "deepseek/deepseek-v3.2",
    "generated_result": {
      "generated_answer": "We need to find the smallest width \\( W \\) such that the given \\( N \\) words, with widths \\( L_i \\), can be arranged in at most \\( M \\) lines, with a space of width 1 between words on the same line.\n\n---\n\n## Problem restatement\n\nWe have words of given widths \\( L_1, L_2, \\dots, L_N \\) (all integers ≥ 1).  \nThey must be placed in order, each word either:\n\n- On the same line as the previous word, with a space of width 1 between them.\n- Or on the next line, starting at the left edge.\n\nA line’s width is the sum of widths of words on that line plus the spaces between them (number of spaces = number of words on that line − 1).  \nWe cannot exceed width \\( W \\) for any line.\n\nWe want the smallest \\( W \\) such that the sentence fits in ≤ \\( M \\) lines.\n\n---\n\n## Observations\n\nThis is similar to *word wrapping* / *line breaking* problem, but here we’re not trying to optimize anything other than finding minimal \\( W \\) for given \\( M \\).\n\nWe can **check** for a given \\( W \\) whether it’s possible to fit into \\( M \\) lines by greedy packing: put as many words as possible on a line without exceeding \\( W \\).\n\nBut we want **minimum** \\( W \\), so we can **binary search** on \\( W \\).\n\n---\n\n### Feasibility check for a given \\( W \\)\n\nLet’s try greedy:\n\nStart new line, put first word there.  \nAdd next word if current line width + 1 + \\( L_{\\text{next}} \\) ≤ \\( W \\).  \nIf not possible, start new line.\n\nCount lines used. If ≤ \\( M \\), feasible.\n\nThis greedy is optimal for minimizing lines for fixed \\( W \\) because it’s the standard “fill as much as possible” approach.\n\n---\n\n### Range for binary search\n\nLower bound:  \nWe must fit all words. Even if all on one line, width = sum(\\( L_i \\)) + (N−1) spaces.  \nBut since we have ≤ \\( M \\) lines, maybe max word width is lower bound? Actually, the minimal possible \\( W \\) is at least the max of \\( L_i \\) because a single word must fit on a line.\n\nBut better:  \nWe can take lower bound as max( \\( L_i \\) ), because any line containing a word of width \\( L_i \\) must have width ≥ \\( L_i \\).\n\nUpper bound:  \nIf we put all words on one line: total width = sum(\\( L_i \\)) + (N−1). This is definitely enough, but maybe less is enough. For binary search upper bound, we can use that.\n\nBut since \\( L_i \\) can be huge (up to \\( 10^9 \\)) and N up to \\( 2\\times 10^5 \\), sum could be huge (up to \\( 2\\times 10^{14} \\) + spaces). So we need to handle large integers.\n\n---\n\n### Binary search bounds:\n\nLower bound: \\( \\max(L_i) \\)  \nUpper bound: \\( \\sum L_i + (N-1) \\) (all words in one line)\n\nWe search smallest \\( W \\) in [low, high] where greedy uses ≤ \\( M \\) lines.\n\n---\n\n### Greedy algorithm details:\n\nLet current_line_width = 0  \nLet lines_used = 1 initially (first line)  \nFor each word width \\( w \\):\n\nIf current_line_width + w ≤ \\( W \\) and current_line_width == 0 (first word on line), add it without space.  \nIf current_line_width > 0, then adding needs a space: check current_line_width + 1 + w ≤ \\( W \\).\n\nIf not possible, start new line: lines_used++, current_line_width = w.  \nIf possible, current_line_width += 1 + w.\n\n---\n\n## Implementation plan\n\n1. Read N, M, list L.\n2. Compute low = max(L), high = sum(L) + N − 1.\n3. Binary search on W.\n4. Check function: greedy pack into lines, count lines.\n5. Output minimal W.\n\n---\n\n### Edge cases:\n\n- M ≥ N: then each word on separate line: W must be ≥ max(L), so minimal W = max(L).\n- M = 1: all in one line: W = sum(L) + N − 1.\n- Large values: use Python’s int.\n\n---\n\nLet’s write the code.\n\n---\n\n```python\nimport sys\n\ndef can_fit(W, L, M):\n    lines_used = 1\n    current_width = L[0]\n    for i in range(1, len(L)):\n        # try to put on same line\n        if current_width + 1 + L[i] <= W:\n            current_width += 1 + L[i]\n        else:\n            lines_used += 1\n            if lines_used > M:\n                return False\n            current_width = L[i]\n    return lines_used <= M\n\ndef main():\n    input = sys.stdin.read().strip().split()\n    N = int(input[0])\n    M = int(input[1])\n    L = list(map(int, input[2:]))\n\n    low = max(L)\n    high = sum(L) + N - 1\n\n    while low < high:\n        mid = (low + high) // 2\n        if can_fit(mid, L, M):\n            high = mid\n        else:\n            low = mid + 1\n    print(low)\n\nif __name__ == \"__main__\":\n    main()\n```",
