    "prompt": "Generate an executable Python function generated from the given prompt. The function should take stdin as input and print the output. Simply call the function after the definition. Question:\nTakahashi is displaying a sentence with N words in a window.\r\nAll words have the same height, and the width of the i-th word (1\\leq i\\leq N) is L _ i.\nThe words are displayed in the window separated by a space of width 1.\r\nMore precisely, when the sentence is displayed in a window of width W, the following conditions are satisfied.\n\n- The sentence is divided into several lines.\n- The first word is displayed at the beginning of the top line.\n- The i-th word (2\\leq i\\leq N) is displayed either with a gap of 1 after the (i-1)-th word, or at the beginning of the line below the line containing the (i-1)-th word. It will not be displayed anywhere else.\n- The width of each line does not exceed W. Here, the width of a line refers to the distance from the left end of the leftmost word to the right end of the rightmost word.\n\nWhen Takahashi displayed the sentence in the window, the sentence fit into M or fewer lines.\r\nFind the minimum possible width of the window.\n\nInput\n\nThe input is given from Standard Input in the following format:\nN M\r\nL _ 1 L _ 2 \\ldots L _ N\n\nOutput\n\nPrint the answer in one line.\n\nConstraints\n\n\n- 1\\leq M\\leq N\\leq2\\times10 ^ 5\n- 1\\leq L _ i\\leq10^9\\ (1\\leq i\\leq N)\n- All input values are integers.\n\nSample Input 1\n\n13 3\r\n9 5 2 7 1 8 8 2 1 5 2 3 6\n\nSample Output 1\n\n26\r\n\nWhen the width of the window is 26, you can fit the given sentence into three lines as follows.\n\nYou cannot fit the given sentence into three lines when the width of the window is 25 or less, so print 26.\nNote that you should not display a word across multiple lines, let the width of a line exceed the width of the window, or rearrange the words.\n\nSample Input 2\n\n10 1\r\n1000000000 1000000000 1000000000 1000000000 1000000000 1000000000 1000000000 1000000000 1000000000 1000000000\n\nSample Output 2\n\n10000000009\r\n\nNote that the answer may not fit into a 32\\operatorname{{bit}} integer.\n\nSample Input 3\n\n30 8\r\n8 55 26 97 48 37 47 35 55 5 17 62 2 60 23 99 73 34 75 7 46 82 84 29 41 32 31 52 32 60\n\nSample Output 3\n\n189",
    "prediction": "qwen/qwen3-235b-a22b-2507",
    "generated_result": {
      "generated_answer": "We are given:\n\n- `N`: the number of words\n- `M`: the maximum number of lines allowed\n- `L[i]`: the width of the i-th word\n\nWe need to **find the minimum window width W** such that all words can be arranged in **at most M lines**, following these rules:\n\n- Words go left to right, in order.\n- Words are separated by a space of width 1 (so if two words are on the same line, there's a 1-unit gap between them).\n- A word cannot be split across lines.\n- The line width is from the **left end of the first word** to the **right end of the last word** on that line.\n\nThus, for a line containing words with widths `[l1, l2, ..., lk]`, the total line width is:\n\n```\nline_width = l1 + 1 + l2 + 1 + l3 + ... + 1 + lk\n           = l1 + l2 + ... + lk + (k - 1)\n```\n\nWe want to find the **minimum W** such that we can split the sequence of words into **≤ M lines** with no line width exceeding W.\n\n---\n\n### Strategy\n\nThis is a classic **binary search on answer** problem.\n\nWe can binary search on W (window width):\n\n- **Lower bound**: at least the maximum word width (since each individual word must fit on a line).\n- **Upper bound**: worst case, all words in one line → sum of all L[i] + (N - 1) spaces.\n\nThen, for a given candidate W, we check whether it's possible to fit all words into ≤ M lines.\n\nThe feasibility function:\n\n- Greedily pack words into lines.\n- Start a new line when adding the next word (with a space + word) would exceed W.\n\n---\n\n### Implementation\n\nWe'll:\n1. Read input.\n2. Define a function to check if a given width `W` can display the sentence in ≤ M lines.\n3. Binary search between `lo = max(L)` and `hi = sum(L) + N - 1`.\n\nNote: Since `L[i]` can be up to `10^9` and `N` up to `2e5`, the total sum may be up to `2e5 * 10^9 = 2e14`, so we use integers (Python int is fine).\n\n---\n\nLet’s code accordingly.\n\n```python\nimport sys\n\ndef solve():\n    data = sys.stdin.read().split()\n    n = int(data[0])\n    m = int(data[1])\n    L = list(map(int, data[2:2+n]))\n    \n    # If only one word per line\n    min_width = max(L)  # At least the widest word must fit\n    max_width = sum(L) + n - 1  # All words on one line: total length + (n-1) spaces\n    \n    def can_fit(W):\n        lines = 1\n        current_line_width = 0  # current line's total width (from first word left to last word right)\n        for i, l in enumerate(L):\n            if i == 0:\n                # First word on the line: just place it\n                current_line_width = l\n            else:\n                # Need a space (1) and then the word (l)\n                if current_line_width + 1 + l <= W:\n                    current_line_width += 1 + l\n                else:\n                    # Start new line\n                    lines += 1\n                    current_line_width = l\n                    if lines > m:\n                        return False\n        return True\n    \n    lo, hi = min_width, max_width\n    ans = hi\n    while lo <= hi:\n        mid = (lo + hi) // 2\n        if can_fit(mid):\n            ans = mid\n            hi = mid - 1\n        else:\n            lo = mid + 1\n            \n    print(ans)\n\nsolve()\n```",
