    "global index": "LiveCodeBench_169",
    "prompt": "Generate an executable Python function generated from the given prompt. Return the function body without invoking it at the final solution. Question:\nYou are given an array of positive integers nums of length n.\nA polygon is a closed plane figure that has at least 3 sides. The longest side of a polygon is smaller than the sum of its other sides.\nConversely, if you have k (k >= 3) positive real numbers a_1, a_2, a_3, ..., a_k where a_1 <= a_2 <= a_3 <= ... <= a_k and a_1 + a_2 + a_3 + ... + a_k-1 > a_k, then there always exists a polygon with k sides whose lengths are a_1, a_2, a_3, ..., a_k.\nThe perimeter of a polygon is the sum of lengths of its sides.\nReturn the largest possible perimeter of a polygon whose sides can be formed from nums, or -1 if it is not possible to create a polygon.\n \nExample 1:\n\nInput: nums = [5,5,5]\nOutput: 15\nExplanation: The only possible polygon that can be made from nums has 3 sides: 5, 5, and 5. The perimeter is 5 + 5 + 5 = 15.\n\nExample 2:\n\nInput: nums = [1,12,1,2,5,50,3]\nOutput: 12\nExplanation: The polygon with the largest perimeter which can be made from nums has 5 sides: 1, 1, 2, 3, and 5. The perimeter is 1 + 1 + 2 + 3 + 5 = 12.\nWe cannot have a polygon with either 12 or 50 as the longest side because it is not possible to include 2 or more smaller sides that have a greater sum than either of them.\nIt can be shown that the largest possible perimeter is 12.\n\nExample 3:\n\nInput: nums = [5,5,50]\nOutput: -1\nExplanation: There is no possible way to form a polygon from nums, as a polygon has at least 3 sides and 50 > 5 + 5.\n\n \nConstraints:\n\n3 <= n <= 10^5\n1 <= nums[i] <= 10^9",
    "prediction": "deepseek/deepseek-v3.2",
    "generated_result": {
      "generated_answer": "To solve this problem, we want the largest possible perimeter of a polygon that can be formed from the given numbers.  \nThe key rule: for a set of sides \\( a_1 \\leq a_2 \\leq \\dots \\leq a_k \\), they can form a polygon if and only if  \n\n\\[\na_1 + a_2 + \\dots + a_{k-1} > a_k\n\\]\n\nThat means the largest side must be smaller than the sum of the other sides.\n\n---\n\n## Thought process\n\nIf we sort the array, the largest possible polygon will likely use the largest numbers available (because perimeter = sum of all chosen sides).  \nBut we must satisfy the polygon inequality for the chosen set.\n\nA good approach:\n1. Sort the array.\n2. Start from the largest numbers and try to build valid polygons greedily.\n3. For any candidate set of \\( k \\) sides (the largest \\( k \\) numbers), check if the largest is smaller than the sum of the rest.\n4. Since we want the largest perimeter, we can start from the end of the sorted array and check backwards.\n\nA known trick:  \nIf we pick the largest \\( m \\) numbers \\( x_1 \\leq x_2 \\leq \\dots \\leq x_m \\),  \nthe polygon condition is \\( x_1 + \\dots + x_{m-1} > x_m \\).  \nIf this fails, \\( x_m \\) is too large, so we drop it and check the next largest set.\n\nWe can check this efficiently by accumulating the sum from smallest to largest in the sorted list:\n- Keep a running total of all elements except the current largest candidate.\n- At each step, if the running total > current largest, we found a valid polygon starting from there.\n- Since we want the largest perimeter, we should start checking from the end.\n\n---\n\n**Algorithm:**\n1. Sort `nums`.\n2. Let `total` = sum of all numbers.\n3. Iterate from last element to the third element (since we need at least 3 sides):\n   - Subtract current largest from total (so `total` now represents sum of all previous elements).\n   - If `total > current largest`, return `total + current largest` as perimeter (because perimeter = sum of all chosen sides = total + current largest).\n4. If loop ends, return -1.\n\nWait — careful:  \nWe want the largest perimeter, so we should start from largest numbers and go backwards, checking if the sum of all previous is greater than current.\n\nActually, simpler:\nLet sorted array be `a[0..n-1]`.  \nWe check from the end: at index `i` (where `i >= 2`), we consider `a[i]` as largest side, and sum of `a[0..i-1]` as sum of others.  \nIf `sum(a[0..i-1]) > a[i]`, then all from `0..i` can form a polygon, perimeter = `sum(a[0..i])`.  \nWe start from the largest `i` and go down to find the first valid one — that’s the largest perimeter.\n\n---\n\n**Optimization:**  \nWe can compute prefix sums to get `sum(a[0..i-1])` quickly.\n\n---\n\nHere’s the Python function:\n\n```python\ndef largest_perimeter(nums):\n    nums.sort()\n    prefix_sum = 0\n    total_sum = sum(nums)\n    \n    # We'll check from the end\n    for i in range(len(nums) - 1, 1, -1):\n        # sum of elements before nums[i] = total_sum - nums[i]\n        if total_sum - nums[i] > nums[i]:\n            return total_sum\n        total_sum -= nums[i]\n    return -1\n```",
