      "generated_answer": "```python\ndef maximumSum(nums, k):\n    \"\"\"\n    Calculates the maximum possible sum of squares of k elements from the transformed array.\n    \n    The core observation is that the bitwise AND/OR operation preserves the total count of set bits\n    for each bit position across the array. To maximize the sum of squares, we want to concentrate\n    these bit counts into the numerically largest possible values. Since x^2 is convex, having\n    some very large numbers yields a higher sum than having distributed values.\n    \n    Therefore, the strategy is:\n    1. Count the occurrence of every bit position (0 to 29) across all numbers in nums.\n    2. Reconstruct the optimal array of length N (or just top k) where for each bit position,\n       the '1's are placed in the first `count` elements of the maintained sorted values.\n       This creates a scenario where the first `k` numbers have the maximum possible value\n       by greedily taking available bits.\n    3. Compute the sum of squares of the top `k` values modulo 10^9 + 7.\n    \n    Time Complexity: O(30 * N + 30 * k) which is O(N) for given constraints.\n    Space Complexity: O(N) for bit counts and the result array.\n    \"\"\"\n    MOD = 10**9 + 7\n    n = len(nums)\n    \n    # Counts for each bit position (0 to 29 covers up to 10^9)\n    counts = [0] * 30\n    \n    for num in nums:\n        for i in range(30):\n            if (num >> i) & 1:\n                counts[i] += 1\n                \n    # We only need the top k values. \n    # Initialize an array to store the values of the top k numbers.\n    # Since the construction naturally yields non-increasing values (most bits go to index 0, fewer to index 1...),\n    # this array represents the top k elements directly.\n    top_k_vals = [0] * k\n    \n    for i in range(30):\n        bit_val = 1 << i\n        limit = counts[i] if counts[i] < k else k\n        \n        for j in range(limit):\n            top_k_vals[j] += bit_val\n            \n    # Calculate the sum of squares modulo 10^9 + 7\n    max_sum_sq = 0\n    for val in top_k_vals:\n        max_sum_sq = (max_sum_sq + val * val) % MOD\n        \n    return max_sum_sq\n```",
