      "generated_answer": "The problem asks us to count the number of people covered by at least one insurance policy.\nWe are given a family tree where each person $i$ ($i \\ge 2$) has a parent $p_i$. This implies a rooted tree structure with root 1. We are given $M$ insurance policies, each starting at a person $x_i$ covering that person and their descendants up to $y_i$ generations deep.\nSpecifically, an insurance at $x_i$ with depth $y_i$ covers all nodes $v$ such that $x_i$ is an ancestor of $v$ (possibly itself) and the distance $dist(x_i, v) \\le y_i$.\nIn the tree rooted at 1, the distance is $depth(v) - depth(x_i)$. The condition $depth(v) - depth(x_i) \\le y_i$ is equivalent to $depth(v) \\le depth(x_i) + y_i$.\n\nThus, a person $v$ is covered if there exists at least one insurance $i$ such that $x_i$ is an ancestor of $v$ and $depth(v) \\le depth(x_i) + y_i$.\nThis condition can be rewritten as: $\\max_{i: x_i \\in Ancestors(v)} (depth(x_i) + y_i) \\ge depth(v)$.\n\nWe can compute this efficiently by iterating through the nodes. Since the problem constraints state $p_i \\le i-1$, the parent of node $i$ is always a node with a smaller index. This means we can process nodes in the order $1, 2, \\ldots, N$, and for each node $i$, the maximum cover depth from its ancestors (stored in a variable or array) will already be computed.\n\nWe maintain an array `max_y[u]` which stores the maximum $y$ for any insurance starting at node $u$. If no insurance starts at $u$, `max_y[u]` is $-1$.\nWe also maintain `depth[u]`, the distance from the root.\nThen we compute `limit[u]`, which represents the maximum `depth(x) + y` for any insurance $x$ on the path from the root to $u$ (inclusive).\n`limit[1] = depth[1] + max_y[1]`.\nFor $i > 1$, `limit[i] = max(limit[P[i]], depth[i] + max_y[i])`.\nNote that if `max_y[i]` is -1, `depth[i] + max_y[i]` will be `depth[i] - 1`, which is strictly less than `depth[i]`, so it won't count as a valid cover for node $i$ itself, nor does it artificially inflate the limit compared to the requirement.\nNode $i$ is covered if `limit[i] \\ge depth[i]`.\n\nThis approach works in $O(N + M)$ time using an iterative method to avoid recursion depth issues.\n\n```python\nimport sys\n\n# Increase recursion depth just in case, though iterative approach is used.\nsys.setrecursionlimit(300005)\n\ndef solve():\n    # Read all input from stdin\n    input_data = sys.stdin.read().split()\n    \n    if not input_data:\n        return\n\n    iterator = iter(input_data)\n    \n    try:\n        N = int(next(iterator))\n        M = int(next(iterator))\n    except StopIteration:\n        return\n\n    # Parents array. P[i] will store the parent of person i.\n    # Person 1 has no parent (index 0 in P array is unused or 0).\n    P = [0] * (N + 1)\n    for i in range(2, N + 1):\n        P[i] = int(next(iterator))\n    \n    # max_y array stores the max y for insurances starting at node i.\n    # Initialize with -1 to represent no insurance.\n    max_y = [-1] * (N + 1)\n    \n    for _ in range(M):\n        try:\n            x = int(next(iterator))\n            y = int(next(iterator))\n        except StopIteration:\n            break\n        \n        if y > max_y[x]:\n            max_y[x] = y\n            \n    # Compute depths.\n    # Constraint p_i <= i-1 ensures topological order 1..N\n    depth = [0] * (N + 1)\n    # depth[1] is 0 by default\n    \n    for i in range(2, N + 1):\n        depth[i] = depth[P[i]] + 1\n        \n    # Compute coverage count.\n    # M_limit[i] stores the coverage limit provided by the best insurance on the path from root to i.\n    # M_limit[i] = max( M_limit[P[i]], depth[i] + max_y[i] )\n    \n    M_limit = [-1] * (N + 1)\n    ans = 0\n    \n    # Node 1 (Root)\n    self_limit = depth[1] + max_y[1] if max_y[1] != -1 else -1\n    M_limit[1] = self_limit\n    if self_limit >= depth[1]:\n        ans += 1\n    \n    for i in range(2, N + 1):\n        # Parent limit\n        parent_limit = M_limit[P[i]]\n        \n        # Self limit\n        # If no insurance, max_y[i] is -1.\n        self_limit = depth[i] + max_y[i]\n        \n        # Max of parent and self\n        current_limit = parent_limit\n        if self_limit > current_limit:\n            current_limit = self_limit\n            \n        M_limit[i] = current_limit\n        \n        if current_limit >= depth[i]:\n            ans += 1\n            \n    print(ans)\n\nif __name__ == '__main__':\n    solve()\n```",
