    "generated_result": {
      "generated_answer": "We can think about this problem as finding the largest window of sorted elements that can be made equal using at most `k` operations.\n\nIf we sort the array, then making a contiguous window of elements equal is more efficient.  \nFor a sorted array, to make all elements in a window `[left, right]` equal to some target within the window, the cheapest target is the median (or any element in the window, but median minimizes adjustments).  \nWe can check feasibility using prefix sums to compute the total cost quickly.\n\nHere’s a step-by-step reasoning:\n\n1. Sort `nums`.\n2. Use a sliding window `[left, right]` to find the largest window where making all elements equal to the median costs at most `k`.\n3. The cost to make all elements in `[left, right]` equal to `nums[mid]` (where `mid = (left + right) // 2`) can be computed quickly using prefix sums:\n   - Left side cost: `nums[mid] * (mid - left) - sum(nums[left:mid])`\n   - Right side cost: `sum(nums[mid+1:right+1]) - nums[mid] * (right - mid)`\n   - Total = left_cost + right_cost.\n4. If total <= k, expand right. Else, move left.\n5. The window size (`right - left + 1`) is the possible frequency.\n\nHere’s the executable Python function:\n\n```python\ndef maxFrequency(nums, k):\n    nums.sort()\n    n = len(nums)\n    prefix = [0] * (n + 1)\n    for i in range(n):\n        prefix[i + 1] = prefix[i] + nums[i]\n    \n    left = 0\n    ans = 1\n    \n    def cost(left, right):\n        mid = (left + right) // 2\n        left_cost = nums[mid] * (mid - left) - (prefix[mid] - prefix[left])\n        right_cost = (prefix[right + 1] - prefix[mid + 1]) - nums[mid] * (right - mid)\n        return left_cost + right_cost\n    \n    for right in range(n):\n        while cost(left, right) > k:\n            left += 1\n        ans = max(ans, right - left + 1)\n    \n    return ans\n```",
