    "global index": "LiveCodeBench_405",
    "prompt": "Generate an executable Python function generated from the given prompt. The function should take stdin as input and print the output. Simply call the function after the definition. Question:\nThere is a grid of H rows and W columns, each cell having a side length of 1, and we have N tiles.\nThe i-th tile (1\\leq i\\leq N) is a rectangle of size A_i\\times B_i.\nDetermine whether it is possible to place the tiles on the grid so that all of the following conditions are satisfied:\n\n- Every cell is covered by exactly one tile.\n- It is fine to have unused tiles.\n- The tiles may be rotated or flipped when placed. However, each tile must be aligned with the edges of the cells without extending outside the grid.\n\nInput\n\nThe input is given from Standard Input in the following format:\nN H W\nA_1 B_1\nA_2 B_2\n\\ldots\nA_N B_N\n\nOutput\n\nIf it is possible to place the tiles on the grid so that all of the conditions in the problem statement are satisfied, print Yes; otherwise, print No.\n\nConstraints\n\n\n- 1\\leq N\\leq 7\n- 1 \\leq H,W \\leq 10\n- 1\\leq A_i,B_i\\leq 10\n- All input values are integers.\n\nSample Input 1\n\n5 5 5\n1 1\n3 3\n4 4\n2 3\n2 5\n\nSample Output 1\n\nYes\n\nPlacing the 2-nd, 4-th, and 5-th tiles as shown below covers every cell of the grid by exactly one tile.\n\nHence, print Yes.\n\nSample Input 2\n\n1 1 2\n2 3\n\nSample Output 2\n\nNo\n\nIt is impossible to place the tile without letting it extend outside the grid.\nHence, print No.\n\nSample Input 3\n\n1 2 2\n1 1\n\nSample Output 3\n\nNo\n\nIt is impossible to cover all cells with the tile.\nHence, print No.\n\nSample Input 4\n\n5 3 3\n1 1\n2 2\n2 2\n2 2\n2 2\n\nSample Output 4\n\nNo\n\nNote that each cell must be covered by exactly one tile.",
    "prediction": "deepseek/deepseek-v3.2",
    "generated_result": {
      "generated_answer": "We need to check if we can tile an \\( H \\times W \\) grid completely with given rectangles, possibly rotated, without overlap and without leaving gaps, allowing unused tiles.  \n\nGiven small constraints (\\(N \\leq 7\\), \\(H, W \\leq 10\\)), we can solve this by **backtracking search** over the grid, trying to place tiles one by one.\n\n---\n\n## Thought process\n\n1. **Representation**  \n   Grid: 2D array of size \\(H \\times W\\), marking empty cells as 0, filled cells with tile ID.  \n   Tiles: each tile is \\((A_i, B_i)\\) but can also be \\((B_i, A_i)\\) if rotated.\n\n2. **Search strategy**  \n   - Find the first empty cell (top-leftmost empty cell) in row-major order.\n   - Try each unused tile in both orientations.\n   - Check if placing it there (with top-left corner at that cell) is valid (fits inside grid and covers only empty cells).\n   - If valid, mark those cells as filled, recurse.\n   - If recursion succeeds, return True.\n   - Backtrack: unmark cells, try next tile.\n\n3. **Optimizations**  \n   - Sort tiles maybe, but not strictly necessary here given small \\(N\\).\n   - Symmetry: we can try both orientations for each tile.\n   - Prune early if remaining area not divisible by tile area? Not exactly, because we can leave tiles unused.\n\n4. **Base case**  \n   If no empty cells remain, we have tiled everything → success.\n\n---\n\n## Implementation plan\n\nWe’ll implement a recursive backtracking function `dfs(pos)` where `pos` is the next cell index (flattened) to fill.\n\nBut simpler: find first empty cell coordinates \\((r, c)\\) each time.\n\n---\n\nHere’s the Python code implementing this:\n\n```python\nimport sys\n\ndef solve():\n    input = sys.stdin.read\n    data = input().split()\n    idx = 0\n    \n    N = int(data[idx]); idx += 1\n    H = int(data[idx]); idx += 1\n    W = int(data[idx]); idx += 1\n    \n    tiles = []\n    for _ in range(N):\n        a = int(data[idx]); idx += 1\n        b = int(data[idx]); idx += 1\n        tiles.append((a, b))\n    \n    grid = [[0] * W for _ in range(H)]\n    \n    def find_empty():\n        for r in range(H):\n            for c in range(W):\n                if grid[r][c] == 0:\n                    return (r, c)\n        return None\n    \n    def can_place(r, c, h, w):\n        if r + h > H or c + w > W:\n            return False\n        for dr in range(h):\n            for dc in range(w):\n                if grid[r + dr][c + dc] != 0:\n                    return False\n        return True\n    \n    def place(r, c, h, w, tile_id):\n        for dr in range(h):\n            for dc in range(w):\n                grid[r + dr][c + dc] = tile_id\n    \n    def remove(r, c, h, w):\n        for dr in range(h):\n            for dc in range(w):\n                grid[r + dr][c + dc] = 0\n    \n    used = [False] * N\n    \n    def backtrack():\n        pos = find_empty()\n        if pos is None:\n            return True\n        r, c = pos\n        \n        for i in range(N):\n            if not used[i]:\n                used[i] = True\n                a, b = tiles[i]\n                \n                # Try orientation (a, b)\n                if can_place(r, c, a, b):\n                    place(r, c, a, b, i+1)\n                    if backtrack():\n                        return True\n                    remove(r, c, a, b)\n                \n                # Try orientation (b, a) if different\n                if a != b:\n                    if can_place(r, c, b, a):\n                        place(r, c, b, a, i+1)\n                        if backtrack():\n                            return True\n                        remove(r, c, b, a)\n                \n                used[i] = False\n        return False\n    \n    if backtrack():\n        print(\"Yes\")\n    else:\n        print(\"No\")\n\nif __name__ == \"__main__\":\n    solve()\n```",
