    "global index": "LiveCodeBench_405",
    "prompt": "Generate an executable Python function generated from the given prompt. The function should take stdin as input and print the output. Simply call the function after the definition. Question:\nThere is a grid of H rows and W columns, each cell having a side length of 1, and we have N tiles.\nThe i-th tile (1\\leq i\\leq N) is a rectangle of size A_i\\times B_i.\nDetermine whether it is possible to place the tiles on the grid so that all of the following conditions are satisfied:\n\n- Every cell is covered by exactly one tile.\n- It is fine to have unused tiles.\n- The tiles may be rotated or flipped when placed. However, each tile must be aligned with the edges of the cells without extending outside the grid.\n\nInput\n\nThe input is given from Standard Input in the following format:\nN H W\nA_1 B_1\nA_2 B_2\n\\ldots\nA_N B_N\n\nOutput\n\nIf it is possible to place the tiles on the grid so that all of the conditions in the problem statement are satisfied, print Yes; otherwise, print No.\n\nConstraints\n\n\n- 1\\leq N\\leq 7\n- 1 \\leq H,W \\leq 10\n- 1\\leq A_i,B_i\\leq 10\n- All input values are integers.\n\nSample Input 1\n\n5 5 5\n1 1\n3 3\n4 4\n2 3\n2 5\n\nSample Output 1\n\nYes\n\nPlacing the 2-nd, 4-th, and 5-th tiles as shown below covers every cell of the grid by exactly one tile.\n\nHence, print Yes.\n\nSample Input 2\n\n1 1 2\n2 3\n\nSample Output 2\n\nNo\n\nIt is impossible to place the tile without letting it extend outside the grid.\nHence, print No.\n\nSample Input 3\n\n1 2 2\n1 1\n\nSample Output 3\n\nNo\n\nIt is impossible to cover all cells with the tile.\nHence, print No.\n\nSample Input 4\n\n5 3 3\n1 1\n2 2\n2 2\n2 2\n2 2\n\nSample Output 4\n\nNo\n\nNote that each cell must be covered by exactly one tile.",
    "prediction": "qwen/qwen3.5-9b",
    "generated_result": {
      "generated_answer": "```python\nimport sys\n\n# Increase recursion depth just in case, though the depth is small (<= 7)\nsys.setrecursionlimit(2000)\n\ndef solve():\n    # Read all input from stdin\n    input_data = sys.stdin.read().split()\n    if not input_data:\n        return\n    \n    iterator = iter(input_data)\n    try:\n        N = int(next(iterator))\n        H = int(next(iterator))\n        W = int(next(iterator))\n        A = []\n        B = []\n        for _ in range(N):\n            A.append(int(next(iterator)))\n            B.append(int(next(iterator)))\n    except StopIteration:\n        return\n\n    # Iterate over all subsets of tiles (2^N possibilities)\n    # N <= 7, so 2^7 = 128 combinations, which is small enough.\n    for mask in range(1, 1 << N):\n        total_area = 0\n        subset_indices = []\n        for i in range(N):\n            if (mask >> i) & 1:\n                total_area += A[i] * B[i]\n                subset_indices.append(i)\n        \n        # If the area of the selected tiles does not match the grid area, skip\n        if total_area != H * W:\n            continue\n            \n        # Check if current subset can tile the grid\n        if check_tiling(H, W, subset_indices, A, B):\n            print(\"Yes\")\n            return\n\n    print(\"No\")\n\ndef check_tiling(H, W, subset_indices, A, B):\n    # Create a grid initialized to 0 (empty)\n    grid = [[0] * W for _ in range(H)]\n    return backtrack(0, grid, subset_indices, A, B, H, W)\n\ndef backtrack(used_mask, grid, subset_indices, A, B, H, W):\n    # Find the top-leftmost empty cell (r, c)\n    r = -1\n    c = -1\n    for i in range(H):\n        for j in range(W):\n            if grid[i][j] == 0:\n                r, c = i, j\n                break\n        if r != -1:\n            break\n    \n    # If no empty cell found, the grid is fully covered\n    if r == -1:\n        return True\n    \n    n_subset = len(subset_indices)\n    # Try to place each unused tile from the subset\n    for k in range(n_subset):\n        if (used_mask >> k) & 1:\n            continue\n            \n        i_orig = subset_indices[k]\n        h_tile, w_tile = A[i_orig], B[i_orig]\n        \n        # Try both orientations (original and rotated 90 degrees)\n        dims = [(h_tile, w_tile), (w_tile, h_tile)]\n        for ph, pw in dims:\n            # Check if tile fits in grid boundaries given top-left at (r, c)\n            # We assume (r, c) is the top-left corner of the tile\n            if r + ph <= H and c + pw <= W:\n                # Check for overlap with already placed tiles\n                if is_valid_placement(grid, r, c, ph, pw, W):\n                    # Place the tile\n                    place_tiles(grid, r, c, ph, pw)\n                    \n                    # Recursively try to cover the rest of the grid\n                    # Mark this tile as used (k)\n                    if backtrack(used_mask | (1 << k), grid, subset_indices, A, B, H, W):\n                        return True\n                    \n                    # Backtrack: remove the tile\n                    unplace_tiles(grid, r, c, ph, pw)\n    \n    return False\n\ndef is_valid_placement(grid, r, c, ph, pw, W):\n    # Check if the proposed placement overlaps with any filled cells\n    for i in range(r, r + ph):\n        for j in range(c, c + pw):\n            if grid[i][j] == 1:\n                return False\n    return True\n\ndef place_tiles(grid, r, c, ph, pw):\n    # Mark cells as occupied\n    for i in range(r, r + ph):\n        for j in range(c, c + pw):\n            grid[i][j] = 1\n\ndef unplace_tiles(grid, r, c, ph, pw):\n    # Unmark cells (backtrack)\n    for i in range(r, r + ph):\n        for j in range(c, c + pw):\n            grid[i][j] = 0\n\nif __name__ == \"__main__\":\n    solve()\n```",
