      "generated_answer": "To solve the problem of determining if it is possible to traverse between all pairs of indices in an array `nums` based on the greatest common divisor (GCD) condition, we can leverage the concept of a graph and use a union-find (disjoint set) data structure to efficiently group connected components.\n\nEach index in the array can be considered a node in a graph, and an edge exists between two nodes `i` and `j` if `gcd(nums[i], nums[j]) > 1`. The goal is to determine if all nodes belong to a single connected component, i.e., there is a path between every pair of nodes.\n\nHere is the Python function that implements the solution using the union-find data structure:\n\n```python\nfrom math import gcd\nfrom collections import defaultdict\n\nclass UnionFind:\n    def __init__(self, n):\n        self.parent = list(range(n))\n        self.rank = [0] * n\n    \n    def find(self, x):\n        if self.parent[x] != x:\n            self.parent[x] = self.find(self.parent[x])\n        return self.parent[x]\n    \n    def union(self, x, y):\n        rootX = self.find(x)\n        rootY = self.find(y)\n        if rootX != rootY:\n            if self.rank[rootX] > self.rank[rootY]:\n                self.parent[rootY] = rootX\n            elif self.rank[rootX] < self.rank[rootY]:\n                self.parent[rootX] = rootY\n            else:\n                self.parent[rootY] = rootX\n                self.rank[rootX] += 1\n\ndef canTraverseAllPairs(nums):\n    n = len(nums)\n    if n == 1:\n        return True\n    \n    # Union-Find structure to hold and process connectivity\n    uf = UnionFind(n)\n    \n    # Map from factors to indices connected by those factors\n    factor_to_indices = defaultdict(list)\n\n    # Helper function to factorize a number\n    def factorize(x):\n        result = []\n        d = 2\n        while d * d <= x:\n            if x % d == 0:\n                result.append(d)\n                while x % d == 0:\n                    x //= d\n            d += 1\n        if x > 1:\n            result.append(x)\n        return result\n    \n    # Populate the factor_to_indices mapping with prime factors as keys\n    for i in range(n):\n        factors = factorize(nums[i])\n        for factor in factors:\n            factor_to_indices[factor].append(i)\n    \n    # Union nodes that share the same factors\n    for indices in factor_to_indices.values():\n        for i in range(1, len(indices)):\n            uf.union(indices[0], indices[i])\n    \n    # Check if all nodes are in the same connected component\n    root = uf.find(0)\n    for i in range(1, n):\n        if uf.find(i) != root:\n            return False\n    \n    return True\n```\n\nHere is a brief explanation of the key components of the solution:\n\n1. **Union-Find Data Structure**: This structure efficiently supports `find` and `union` operations, which help in managing and querying connected components.\n\n2. **Factorization**: Each number in `nums` is factorized into its prime components. Indices of numbers sharing common factors are stored together, since such numbers are directly connected by an edge.\n\n3. **Union-Connected Components**: For each group of indices connected by a common factor, we perform union operations, effectively merging their connected components.\n\n4. **Connected Component Check**: Finally, we check if all indices (nodes in the graph) belong to the same connected component, indicating that traversal is possible between every pair based on the GCD condition.\n\nThis approach efficiently checks connectivity in `O(n log n)` time complexity, assuming fast factorization and union-find operations, which is feasible given constraint limits.",
