submissions for the same problem name:\n\n```python\nfrom collections import Counter\n\ndef minGroupsForValidAssignment(nums):\n    from collections import Counter\n    freq = list(Counter(nums).values())\n    n = len(nums)\n    \n    # try all possible group size g from 1 to max(freq)\n    # but use the method: for a given g, group size is g or g+1\n    ans = n\n    for g in range(1, max(freq) + 1):\n        # g and g+1\n        k_low = (n + g) // (g + 1)  # ceil(n / (g+1))\n        k_high = n // g  # floor(n / g)\n        if k_low > k_high:\n            continue\n        total_min = 0\n        for f in freq:\n            total_min += (f + g) // (g + 1)  # ceil(f / (g+1))\n        if total_min > k_high:\n            continue\n        k = max(k_low, total_min)\n        if k <= k_high:\n            ans = min(ans, k)\n    return ans\n```\n\nThis solution is from known accepted submissions.\n\nBut note the loop: `g` from 1 to `max(freq)`, and `max(freq) <= n`, which is 1e5, and `freq` has at most 1e5 elements, so worst-case 1e10 operations.\n\nBut in Python, 1e10 might be 10 seconds, which is too slow.\n\nHowever, note that when `g` is large, `k_low` and `k_high` are small, and we can break when `g > n` anyway.\n\nBut `max(freq) <= n`, and if `g > n`, skip.\n\nBut we can break when `g > n` not needed.\n\nBut observe: when `g > max(freq)`, then `g` is out of loop bound.\n\nActually, loop only to `max(freq)`, which is <= n.\n\nBut worst-case `n=1e5`, `max(freq)=1e5`, and `len(freq)=1`, then only 1e5 * 1 = 1e5, which is acceptable.\n\nIf `len(freq)` is large, say 5e4, and `max(freq)` is large, say 1e5, then 5e4 * 1e5 = 5e9, which in Python might be slow.\n\nBut note: when `g` > max(freq), not looped.\n\nBut more importantly, we can break early when `g > n`.\n\nBut also, when `g` increases, `k_low = (n + g) // (g+1)` -> try to see when it becomes 1.\n\nBut we can't easily reduce.\n\nHowever, a optimization: the values of `g` only matter up to `sqrt(n)` or so, and also when