    "generated_result": {
      "generated_answer": "```python\nimport sys\n\n# Increase recursion depth just in case (though not needed for this iterative solution)\nsys.setrecursionlimit(2000)\n\ndef solve():\n    # Read all input from stdin efficiently\n    input_data = sys.stdin.read().split()\n    if not input_data:\n        return\n\n    iterator = iter(input_data)\n    \n    try:\n        H = int(next(iterator))\n        W = int(next(iterator))\n        N = int(next(iterator))\n        T = next(iterator)\n        grid_lines = [next(iterator) for _ in range(H)]\n    except StopIteration:\n        return\n\n    # Precompute land_masks\n    # land_masks[r] is an integer where the bit at index c is 1 if cell (r, c) is land ('.')\n    # and 0 if sea ('#').\n    # We use bitwise operations to simulate the movement efficiently.\n    \n    land_masks = []\n    \n    for r in range(H):\n        row_str = grid_lines[r]\n        m = 0\n        for c, char in enumerate(row_str):\n            if char == '.':\n                m |= (1 << c)\n        land_masks.append(m)\n\n    # Initialize current_masks with valid cells (land cells).\n    # The problem asks for the number of starting positions such that the path stays on land.\n    # We maintain the set of \"valid current positions\" after k steps in curr_masks.\n    # Initially, valid positions are all land cells (since step 0 starts at land).\n    # curr_masks[r] is an integer representing the set of valid columns in row r.\n    curr_masks = [m for m in land_masks]\n    \n    # Process each move in T\n    # We update curr_masks based on the move and must intersect with land_masks\n    # to ensure that the new position is also land.\n    # Note: The \"valid path\" condition implies that every cell visited must be land.\n    # By intersecting with land_masks at every step, we ensure the current cell is land.\n    # Since the previous set of cells was already validated to be land (inductive step),\n    # the entire path segment formed by the move is valid.\n    \n    for move in T:\n        if move == 'L':\n            # Move Left: column j -> j-1.\n            # In bitmask: shift right by 1 (Bit j moves to j-1).\n            # Bit 0 (col 0) moves out and is lost.\n            for r in range(H):\n                if curr_masks[r]:\n                    curr_masks[r] &= (curr_masks[r] >> 1) & land_masks[r]\n        elif move == 'R':\n            # Move Right: column j -> j+1.\n            # Bitmask shift left by 1 (Bit j moves to j+1).\n            for r in range(H):\n                if curr_masks[r]:\n                    curr_masks[r] &= (curr_masks[r] << 1) & land_masks[r]\n        elif move == 'U':\n            # Move Up: row r -> r-1.\n            # Columns stay the same, row index decreases.\n            # We must create a new list because positions move between rows.\n            new_masks = [0] * H\n            # Move from current row r to r-1 (source r, dest r-1)\n            # Iterate source rows downward to avoid index issues?\n            # Actually just iterating 1..H-1 covers all valid moves.\n            for r in range(1, H):\n                # Bits in curr_masks[r] represent columns in row r.\n                # We check if same columns are land in row r-1.\n                new_masks[r-1] = curr_masks[r] & land_masks[r-1]\n            curr_masks = new_masks\n        elif move == 'D':\n            # Move Down: row r -> r+1.\n            new_masks = [0] * H\n            # Move from current row r to r+1 (source r, dest r+1)\n            for r in range(H - 1):\n                new_masks[r+1] = curr_masks[r] & land_masks[r+1]\n            curr_masks = new_masks\n\n    # Count valid positions\n    total_valid = 0\n    for r in range(H):\n        if curr_masks[r] > 0:\n            # Count set bits\n            # bin(x) returns \"0b...\", count '1' gives the number\n            total_valid += bin(curr_masks[r]).count('1')\n            \n    print(total_valid)\n\nif __name__ == '__main__':\n    solve()\n```",
