      "generated_answer": "We can solve this by dynamic programming, where `dp[i]` is the maximum number of jumps to reach index `i`.  \n\nWe’ll initialize `dp[0] = 0` (starting point, no jumps yet), and all others to `-inf` to indicate unreachable.  \nThen, for each `i`, we check all previous indices `j` to see if we can jump from `j` to `i` within the target difference. If yes, we update `dp[i]` to `max(dp[i], dp[j] + 1)`.  \n\nFinally, if `dp[n-1]` is still `-inf`, return `-1`, else return `dp[n-1]`.\n\nHere’s the Python function:\n\n```python\ndef maximumJumps(nums, target):\n    n = len(nums)\n    dp = [-float('inf')] * n\n    dp[0] = 0  # start at index 0 with 0 jumps\n    \n    for i in range(1, n):\n        for j in range(i):\n            diff = nums[i] - nums[j]\n            if -target <= diff <= target:\n                if dp[j] != -float('inf'):\n                    dp[i] = max(dp[i], dp[j] + 1)\n    \n    return dp[n-1] if dp[n-1] != -float('inf') else -1\n```",
