    "prompt": "Please read the following multiple-choice questions and provide the most likely correct answer based on the options given.\n\nContext: None\n\nQuestion: Statement 1| A U2F USB dongle prevents malware on the user’s computer from stealing the user’s second factor to authenticate as that user even when the user’s computer is turned off. Statement 2| A server using U2F can reliably determine that the user who is attempting to login is indeed behind the computer that sent the login request.\n\nOptions: \nA. False, True\nB. True, True\nC. False, False\nD. Both statements are partially true\nE. False, Not enough information\nF. Not enough information, Not enough information\nG. True, False\nH. True, Not enough information\nI. Not enough information, True\nJ. Not enough information, False\n\n\nProvide the correct letter choice in \\boxed{X}, where X is the correct letter choice. Keep the explanation or feedback within 3 sentences.",
    "prediction": "deepseek/deepseek-v3.2",
    "generated_result": {
      "generated_answer": "Let’s examine each statement:  \n\n- **Statement 1** says a U2F dongle prevents malware from stealing the second factor *even when the computer is off*.  \n  This is false — if the computer is off, malware can’t steal anything anyway, but the phrase \"prevents… when the computer is off\" is misleading; U2F prevents theft during active use, but the \"even when turned off\" part makes the overall statement logically flawed.  \n\n- **Statement 2** says a server using U2F can reliably determine the user is indeed behind the computer that sent the login request.  \n  This is true — U2F includes a proof of physical presence by requiring a button press, which confirms the user is present at the time of authentication.  \n\nThat gives us: **False, True** → **Option A**.\n\n\\boxed{A}",
