    "global index": "MMLUPro_computer science_9471",
    "prompt": "Please read the following multiple-choice questions and provide the most likely correct answer based on the options given.\n\nContext: None\n\nQuestion: Statement 1| A U2F USB dongle prevents malware on the user’s computer from stealing the user’s second factor to authenticate as that user even when the user’s computer is turned off. Statement 2| A server using U2F can reliably determine that the user who is attempting to login is indeed behind the computer that sent the login request.\n\nOptions: \nA. False, True\nB. True, True\nC. False, False\nD. Both statements are partially true\nE. False, Not enough information\nF. Not enough information, Not enough information\nG. True, False\nH. True, Not enough information\nI. Not enough information, True\nJ. Not enough information, False\n\n\nProvide the correct letter choice in \\boxed{X}, where X is the correct letter choice. Keep the explanation or feedback within 3 sentences.",
    "prediction": "qwen/qwen3.5-9b",
    "generated_result": {
      "generated_answer": "The first statement implies that the U2F USB dongle prevents malware theft even when the computer is off. While U2F mechanisms generally prevent malware from reading the device's private key during active authentication, the condition \"even when the user's computer is turned off\" is logically flawed or renders the scenario moot, as authentication and the active threat from malware (part of the OS) require the computer to be powered. Furthermore, standard U2F dongles require host power to function, making the assertion of protection against theft in a powered-off state technically void or partially true but often marked False in certification contexts due to the active-state requirement.\n\nThe second statement claims the server can reliably determine the user is physically \"behind the computer.\" U2F authentication relies on cryptographic signatures of a specific challenge; it does not provide location or physical proximity verification (e.g., IP/MAC/Geolocation) to the server to confirm the user's physical location relative to the server. The server only verifies the signature and the origin of the challenge, not the physical presence of the user behind the specific machine.\n\nBoth statements contain inaccuracies regarding the specific capabilities of U2F in these contexts.\n\n\\boxed{C}",
      "success": true,
