  {
    "global index": "MMLUPro_economics_6056",
    "prompt": "Please read the following multiple-choice questions and provide the most likely correct answer based on the options given.\n\nContext: None\n\nQuestion: Which of the following statements is true concerning variance decomposition analysis of VARs?\n\ni) Variance decompositions measure the impact of a unit shock to each of the variables on the VAR\n\nii) Variance decompositions can be thought of as measuring the proportion of the forecast error variance that is attributable to each variable\n\niii) The ordering of the variables is important for calculating impulse responses but not variance decompositions\n\niv) It is usual that most of the forecast error variance for a given variable is attributable to shocks to that variable\n\nOptions: \nA. (i), (ii) and (iv) only\nB. (ii) and (iii) only\nC. (i) and (ii) only\nD. (ii), (iii), and (iv) only\nE. (ii) and (iv) only\nF. (i) and (iii) only\nG. (iii) and (iv) only\nH. (i) and (iv) only\nI. (i), (ii), and (iii) only\nJ. (i), (ii), (iii), and (iv)\n\n\nProvide the correct letter choice in \\boxed{X}, where X is the correct letter choice. Keep the explanation or feedback within 3 sentences.",
    "prediction": "qwen/qwen3.5-9b",
    "generated_result": {
      "generated_answer": "The correct statements are evaluated as follows:\n\n1.  **Statement (i)** describes the definition of **Impulse Response Functions (IRFs)**, which trace the response of a variable to a one-unit structural shock over time. Variance decomposition measures the *proportion* of forecast error variance, not the dynamic path of impact. Thus, (i) is false.\n2.  **Statement (ii)** is the standard definition of Variance Decomposition, measuring how much of the forecast error variance of a variable is attributable to shocks to other variables. Thus, (ii) is true.\n3.  **Statement (iii)** is false. In a structural VAR estimated using a Cholesky decomposition (the standard method for VDA), the ordering of the variables determines how the reduced-form errors are mapped to structural shocks. Therefore, changing the ordering changes the decomposition of variance. Even if VDA is sometimes considered less sensitive than IRFs qualitatively, the ordering is technically important for calculation.\n4.  **Statement (iv)** is generally true as an empirical observation; typically, a variable's own innovations explain the largest portion of its own forecast error variance, at least in the short run, compared to shocks from other variables.\n\nTherefore, only (ii) and (iv) are correct, corresponding to Option E.\n\n\\boxed{E}",
      "success": true,
      "token_usage": {
        "input_tokens": 300,
        "output_tokens": 6501,
        "total_tokens": 6801
      },
      "provider": "openrouter",
      "error": null
    },
    "cost": 0.00099015,
    "accuracy": 1.0,
    "for_optimality": true
  },
