    "global index": "AIME_58",
    "prompt": "Please solve the following mathematical problem step by step. \n\nContext: None\n\nQuestion: Let $\\omega = \\cos\\frac{{2\\pi}}{{7}} + i \\cdot \\sin\\frac{{2\\pi}}{{7}},$ where $i = \\sqrt{{-1}}.$ Find the value of the product\\[\\prod_{{k=0}}^6 \\left(\\omega^{{3k}} + \\omega^k + 1\\right).\\]\n\nProvide your final answer in \\boxed{{}} format, where the content inside the braces is the exact mathematical expression or number. For example: \\boxed{{42}}. Keep your explanation clear, concise, and within 3 sentences.",
    "prediction": "deepseek/deepseek-v3.2",
    "generated_result": {
      "generated_answer": "We are given \\(\\omega = e^{2\\pi i /7}\\) a primitive 7th root of unity, so \\(\\omega^7 = 1\\).\n\nNote \\(\\omega^{3k} + \\omega^{k} + 1 = \\frac{\\omega^{3k}-1}{\\omega^k-1}\\) because \\(\\omega^{3k}-1 = (\\omega^k-1)(\\omega^{2k}+\\omega^k+1)\\) but that's not exactly matching. Instead, observe that \\(x^2+x+1 = \\frac{x^3-1}{x-1}\\) for \\(x \\neq 1\\). Let \\(x = \\omega^k\\). Then \\(\\omega^{2k} + \\omega^{k} + 1 = \\frac{\\omega^{3k}-1}{\\omega^{k}-1}\\) for \\(k \\neq 0 \\pmod 7\\).  \n\nOur given expression has \\(\\omega^{3k} + \\omega^{k} + 1\\), but that is actually \\( \\omega^k + \\omega^{3k} + 1\\). Order doesn't matter, so indeed \\(\\omega^{3k} + \\omega^{k} + 1 = \\omega^{k} + \\omega^{3k} + 1\\) same as \\(\\omega^{k} + \\omega^{3k} + 1\\). Wait — let’s check: The cyclotomic polynomial \\(\\Phi_7(x)= x^6+x^5+x^4+x^3+x^2+x+1 = \\frac{x^7-1}{x-1}\\).  \n\nLet’s instead use the factorization over the reals for \\(x^7-1 = (x-1)(x^6+x^5+x^4+x^3+x^2+x+1)\\) and also \\(x^7-1 = \\prod_{m=0}^6 (x - \\omega^m)\\).  \n\nWe can write each term \\(\\omega^{3k}+\\omega^k+1\\) as \\(1 + \\omega^k + \\omega^{3k}\\). Let’s compute \\(P = \\prod_{k=0}^6 (1+\\omega^k + \\omega^{3k})\\).\n\nFor \\(k=0\\), \\(\\omega^0=1\\), so \\(1+1+1 = 3\\).  \nFor \\(k \\neq 0\\), note that \\(\\omega^{3k}\\) runs through all 7th roots of unity as \\(k=0,\\dots,6\\), but not necessarily in the same order as \\(k\\).\n\nWe can think: Set \\(x = \\omega^k\\), \\(1+x+x^{3} = \\frac{x^7 - x}{x-1}? \\) No, check: Multiply numerator/denominator? Better: Use \\(x^7=1\\) for \\(x=\\omega^k\\). Then \\(1+x+x^{3} = \\frac{(x- \\alpha)(x-\\beta)}{1}\\) maybe too messy.\n\nInstead, note: \\(P = \\prod_{k=0}^6 (1+\\omega^k+\\omega^{3k})\\). Since \\(\\omega^k\\) for \\(k=0,\\dots,6\\) are all roots of \\(x^7-1\\), we can relate to polynomial evaluation.\n\nConsider polynomial \\(f(z) = z^7 - 1 = \\prod_{k=0}^6 (z - \\omega^k)\\).  \nNow substitute \\(z = y\\) i