      "generated_answer": "Let $\\omega$ be a primitive 7th root of unity. The 7 values $\\omega^k$ for $k=0, \\dots, 6$ are the roots of $z^7-1=0$. Let $f(z) = z^3+z+1$. The product is $P = \\prod_{k=0}^6 f(\\omega^k)$.\nPairing complex conjugates, we have $f(1) = 3$, and for $k=1, \\dots, 6$, the terms pair as $f(\\omega^k)$ and $f(\\omega^{7-k})$. For instance, $f(\\omega) = \\omega^3+\\omega+1$ and its conjugate $f(\\omega^6) = \\omega^3+\\omega^{-1}+1$ wait, $\\omega^3 \\to \\omega^4$, $\\omega \\to \\omega^6$. So $\\overline{f(\\omega)} = \\omega^4+\\omega^6+1$. Their product is $(\\omega^3+\\omega+1)(\\omega^4+\\omega^6+1) = \\omega^7+\\omega^2+\\omega^3+\\omega^5+\\omega^7+\\omega+\\omega^4+\\omega^6+1 = 3 + \\sum_{j=1}^6 \\omega^j = 3 + (-1) = 2$. The same logic applies to the other pairs $(k, 7-k)$ as they all yield a product of 2. Thus, the total product is $3 \\cdot 2 \\cdot 2 \\cdot 2 = 24$.\nAlternatively, this product is the resultant of $x^7-1$ and $x^3+x+1$, which is $24$.\n\n\\boxed{24}",
