      "generated_answer": "To solve the problem, we recognize that \\(\\omega = e^{2\\pi i / 7}\\) is a primitive 7th root of unity, satisfying \\(\\omega^7 = 1\\) and \\(\\sum_{k=0}^6 \\omega^k = 0\\). The expression \\(\\omega^{3k} + \\omega^k + 1\\) can be rewritten using symmetry and the properties of roots of unity. Notice that:\n\n\\[ \\omega^{3k} + \\omega^k + 1 = 0 \\]\n\nwill hold for certain values due to being a geometric transformation linked to the roots. Importantly, the polynomial term expansion \\(\\prod_{k=0}^{6} (x-\\omega^k) = x^7-1\\) can be related to the given expression. Evaluating this expression and understanding the symmetry ensures that each factor has exactly two that give zero for half of the terms:\n\n\\[ \\prod_{k=0}^{6} (\\omega^{3k} + \\omega^k + 1) = 7. \\]\n\nHence, the sought value of the product equals \\boxed{7}.",
