    "generated_result": {
      "generated_answer": "The problem asks for the number of integers $N$ between 1 and 1000 for which the Greedy algorithm produces an optimal collection of coins (using denominations 1, 10, 25).\n\nThe Greedy algorithm chooses as many 25-cent coins as possible, then as many 10-cent coins as possible from the remainder, and finally 1-cent coins. Let $q$ be the number of 25-cent coins chosen by the Greedy algorithm, such that $25q \\le N < 25(q+1)$. The remainder is $r = N - 25q$. The algorithm then uses the optimal number of coins for the remainder using only 10-cent and 1-cent coins (since $\\{1, 10\\}$ is a canonical system where Greedy is optimal). The total number of coins used is $C(N) = q + \\lfloor r/10 \\rfloor + (r \\pmod{10})$.\n\nFor a given $N$, the Greedy algorithm fails if there exists another combination of coins using strictly fewer coins. Since 25 is the largest denomination, any alternative optimal solution would necessarily use fewer 25-cent coins than the Greedy algorithm (or the same number, in which case the Greedy solution for the remainder is optimal). Thus, we check if reducing the number of 25-cent coins by 1 yields a solution with fewer coins.\n\nIf we reduce the number of 25-cent coins from $q$ to $q-1$, the remaining value becomes $r + 25$. The cost difference between using $q$ coins of 25-cent and using $q-1$ coins of 25-cent plus coins of denominations 1 and 10 is:\n$$ \\Delta = (q-1 + \\text{Cost}(r+25)) - (q + \\text{Cost}(r)) $$\nwhere $\\text{Cost}(x) = \\lfloor x/10 \\rfloor + (x \\pmod{10})$ is the cost to represent $x$ using 10s and 1s.\n\nLet's analyze $\\text{Cost}(r+25) - \\text{Cost}(r)$. Let $r = 10k + s$, where $s = r \\pmod{10} \\in \\{0, \\dots, 9\\}$.\nIf $s < 5$:\n$$ \\text{Cost}(r) = k + s $$\n$$ \\text{Cost}(r+25) = \\lfloor (10k + s + 25)/10 \\rfloor + (r+25 \\pmod{10}) = (k+2) + (s+5) = k + s + 7 $$\nSo $\\text{Cost}(r+25) = \\text{Cost}(r) + 7$. Then $\\Delta = -1 + 7 = 6 > 0$. Greedy is better.\nIf $s \\ge 5$:\n$$ \\text{Cost}(r) = k + s $$\n$$ \\text{Cost}(r+25) = \\lfloor (10k + s + 25)/10 \\rfloor + (r+25 \\pmod{10}) = (k+3) + (s-5) = k + s - 2 $$\nSo $\\text{Cost}(r+25) = \\text{Cost}(r) - 2$. Then $\\Delta = -1 - 2 = -3 < 0$. Reducing the number of 25-cent coins saves 3 coins. Thus, the Greedy algorithm is suboptimal in this case.\n\nThe Greedy algorithm succeeds if and only if $N \\ge 25$ does NOT satisfy the condition where reducing 25s improves the count. This corresponds to the condition $s < 5$, i.e., $(N \\pmod{25}) \\pmod{10} < 5$. If $N < 25$, the Greedy algorithm always succeeds.\n\nThus, the Greedy algorithm fails for $N$ if $N \\ge 25$ and $(N \\pmod{25}) \\pmod{10} \\ge 5$. Let $r = N \\pmod{25}$. The values of $r \\in \\{0, \\dots, 24\\}$ satisfying $r \\pmod{10} \\ge 5$ are $S_B = \\{5, 6, 7, 8, 9, 15, 16, 17, 18, 19\\}$. There are 10 such remainders.\n\nWe need to count the number of $N \\in \\{1, \\dots, 1000\\}$ that fail this condition. These are the values where $N \\ge 25$ and $N \\pmod{25} \\in S_B$.\nFor each $r \\in S_B$, the values of $N$ are $r, r+25, r+50, \\dots$. The value $N=r$ is $< 25$ (since $r \\in \\{5, \\dots, 19\\} \\subset [1, 24]$) and thus is a Success. The values $N \\in \\{r+25, r+25\\times 2, \\dots \\}$ satisfy $N \\ge 25$.\nThe maximum value is 1000. Since $1000 \\pmod{25} = 0$ and $r \\ge 1$, the terms are $N = r + 25k$.\nFor a fixed $r \\in S_B$, $r + 25k \\le 1000 \\implies 25k \\le 1000 - r \\implies k \\le 39$ (since $r < 25$).\nSo there are 39 failure cases for each of the 10 remainders. Total Failures = $10 \\times 39 =",
