    "generated_result": {
      "generated_answer": "To determine the number of values of \\( N \\) for which the greedy algorithm succeeds, we need to compare the greedy solution to an optimal solution. The greedy algorithm fails when there exists a way to use fewer coins to obtain the same total using a different combination of the available denominations. It is known that the greedy algorithm for U.S. coin denominations (1-cent, 10-cent, 25-cent) fails for values of \\( N \\equiv 3 \\pmod{5} \\) when \\( N \\geq 28 \\), due to its inability to optimally use coins in some cases. For each such \\( N \\), we can use combinations like one fewer 25-cent coin and making up the difference with 10-cent coins and fewer 1-cent coins.\n\nTo find the number of such cases, we note that for \\( N \\geq 28 \\), every fifth number fails the greedy algorithm condition. The numbers from 28 to 1000 that satisfy \\( N \\equiv 3 \\pmod{5} \\) are: 28, 33, 38, ..., 998. This forms an arithmetic sequence where the first term \\( a_1 = 28 \\), common difference \\( d = 5 \\), and last term \\( a_n = 998 \\). The number of terms in the sequence is given by:\n\\[ a_n = a_1 + (n-1)d \\]\n\\[ 998 = 28 + (n-1) \\times 5 \\]\n\\[ 970 = (n-1) \\times 5 \\]\n\\[ n-1 = 194 \\]\n\\[ n = 195 \\]\n\nThus, there are 195 values from 28 to 1000 inclusive for which the greedy algorithm fails. Given there are 1000 integers from 1 to 1000, the number of \\( N \\) values for which the greedy algorithm succeeds is \\( 1000 - 195 = 805 \\).\n\nTherefore, the number of values of \\( N \\) for which the greedy algorithm succeeds is \\(\\boxed{805}\\).",
