      "generated_answer": "The experiment described is the Frye and Edidin experiment (1970), which demonstrated the fluidity of the plasma membrane. To accomplish this, researchers fused the membranes of mouse and human cells, allowing the distinct surface proteins from each to mix and diffuse across the newly merged plasma membrane.\n\nThis process, where two or more distinct cell types are merged into a single cell, is specifically known as **cell fusion**. While this results in the creation of a **hybrid cell** (Option B) or a **heterokaryon** (Option D), the specific *technique* employed to achieve the membrane integration and observation described is the method of cell fusion itself.\n\nTherefore, the most accurate description of the technique employed is Option G.\n\n\\boxed{G}",
