    "global index": "MathQA_436",
    "prompt": "Please read the following multiple-choice questions and provide the most likely correct answer based on the options given.\n\nContext: None\n\nQuestion: a 12 meter long wire is cut into two pieces . if the longer piece is then used to form a perimeter of a square , what is the probability that the area of the square will be more than 4 if the original wire was cut at an arbitrary point ?\n\nOptions: \nA. 1 / 6\nB. 2 / 3\nC. 3 / 10\nD. 1 / 3\nE. 2 / 5\n\n\nProvide the correct letter choice in \\boxed{X}, where X is the correct letter choice. Keep the explanation or feedback within 3 sentences.",
    "prediction": "qwen/qwen3-235b-a22b-2507",
    "generated_result": {
      "generated_answer": "Let the wire be cut at a point $ x $ meters from one end, where $ 0 < x < 12 $. Without loss of generality, assume the longer piece is $ \\max(x, 12 - x) $. Since the longer piece forms the perimeter of a square, its length must be greater than 6 (because if $ x < 6 $, $ 12 - x > 6 $, and vice versa), so the longer piece is always at least 6 meters. The side of the square is $ \\frac{\\text{longer piece}}{4} $, and the area is $ \\left( \\frac{L}{4} \\right)^2 $, where $ L $ is the longer piece.\n\nWe want the area $ > 4 $, so:\n$$\n\\left( \\frac{L}{4} \\right)^2 > 4 \\Rightarrow \\frac{L^2}{16} > 4 \\Rightarrow L^2 > 64 \\Rightarrow L > 8\n$$\nSo the longer piece must be greater than 8 meters. The longer piece is $ > 8 $ when $ x < 4 $ or $ x > 8 $. The total favorable interval is $ 4 $ (from 0 to 4 and 8 to 12), but only the region where the longer piece $ > 8 $ counts. The cut must be in $ [0, 4) $ or $ (8, 12] $, which gives a total length of $ 4 + 4 = 8 $ meters.\n\nBut since the cut is arbitrary along 12 meters, the probability is $ \\frac{8}{12} = \\frac{2}{3} $.\n\n\\boxed{B}",
      "success": true,
      "token_usage": {
        "input_tokens": 162,
        "output_tokens": 377,
        "total_tokens": 539
      },
      "provider": "openrouter",
      "error": null
