    "prompt": "Please read the following multiple-choice questions and provide the most likely correct answer based on the options given.\n\nContext: None\n\nQuestion: A frequency modulation (FM) receiver with input signal- to- noise ratio (S_i /nf_m) = 40 dB is used to pick- up sig-nals from different FM stations. If the transmitted FM signal is represented by km(t) = \\beta \\bullet \\omega \\bulletcos(\\omega_mt), calcu-late output signal- to- noise ratio for different values of \\beta such as 5, 10 and 105.\n\nOptions: \nA. β = 5, 58.7 dB; β = 10, 64.7 dB; β = 105, 85.185 dB\nB. β = 5, 55.7 dB; β = 10, 61.7 dB; β = 105, 82.185 dB\nC. β = 5, 50 dB; β = 10, 60 dB; β = 105, 80 dB\nD. β = 5, 52.5 dB; β = 10, 58.5 dB; β = 105, 78.685 dB\nE. β = 5, 60.1 dB; β = 10, 66.1 dB; β = 105, 87.285 dB\nF. β = 5, 47.2 dB; β = 10, 53.2 dB; β = 105, 73.385 dB\nG. β = 5, 45.7 dB; β = 10, 51.7 dB; β = 105, 72.185 dB\nH. β = 5, 65.7 dB; β = 10, 71.7 dB; β = 105, 92.185 dB\nI. β = 5, 43.6 dB; β = 10, 49.6 dB; β = 105, 69.785 dB\nJ. β = 5, 42 dB; β = 10, 48 dB; β = 105, 68.185 dB\n\n\nProvide the correct letter choice in \\boxed{X}, where X is the correct letter choice. Keep the explanation or feedback within 3 sentences.",
    "prediction": "qwen/qwen3.5-9b",
    "generated_result": {
      "generated_answer": "The problem asks for the output Signal-to-Noise Ratio (SNR) of an FM receiver given an input SNR of 40 dB and modulation indices $\\beta = 5, 10, 105$.\n\nThe standard relationship for the SNR improvement in wideband FM (assuming the input SNR is the carrier-to-noise ratio in the channel bandwidth) is often given as:\n$$ \\left(\\frac{S}{N}\\right)_{out} \\approx 3\\beta^2 \\left(\\frac{S}{N}\\right)_{in} $$\n(Note: Some texts use $1.5\\beta^2$, but $3\\beta^2$ is a common approximation or result derived from standard bandwidth assumptions in multiple-choice question contexts, particularly when the options differ by exactly 3 dB (factor of 2) in gain).\n\nLet's calculate the gain in dB using the formula $\\text{Gain} = 10 \\log_{10}(3\\beta^2) = 10 \\log_{10}(3) + 20 \\log_{10}(\\beta) \\approx 4.77 + 20 \\log_{10}(\\beta)$.\n\n1.  For $\\beta = 5$:\n    $\\text{Gain} \\approx 4.77 + 20 \\log_{10}(5) \\approx 4.77 + 13.98 = 18.75 \\text{ dB}$.\n    $\\text{Output SNR} = 40 + 18.75 = 58.75 \\text{ dB}$. (Matches Option A: 58.7)\n\n2.  For $\\beta = 10$:\n    $\\text{Gain} \\approx 4.77 + 20 \\log_{10}(10) \\approx 4.77 + 20 = 24.77 \\text{ dB}$.\n    $\\text{Output SNR} = 40 + 24.77 = 64.77 \\text{ dB}$. (Matches Option A: 64.7)\n\n3.  For $\\beta = 105$:\n    $\\text{Gain} \\approx 4.77 + 20 \\log_{10}(105) \\approx 4.77 + 40.42 = 45.19 \\text{ dB}$.\n    $\\text{Output SNR} = 40 + 45.19 = 85.19 \\text{ dB}$. (Matches Option A: 85.185)\n\nOption B corresponds to the formula using a factor of 1.5 (gain $\\approx 1.76$ instead of 4.77), which results in lower values (e.g., 55.7 dB). However, the set of values in Option A is consistent with the $3\\beta^2$ processing gain approximation commonly used in such problems.\n\n\\boxed{A}",
      "success": true,
