    "global index": "MusicTheoryBench_189",
    "prompt": "Please read the following multiple-choice questions and provide the most likely correct answer based on the options given.\n\nContext: None\n\nQuestion: Which of the following statements about the whole-tone scale is incorrect?\n\nOptions: \nA. The interval between adjacent notes in the whole-tone scale is a major second\nB. Debussy's piano prelude 'Voiles' made extensive use of the whole-tone scale\nC. If a whole-tone scale has a B flat, it must also contain an E\nD. Within an octave range, the whole-tone scale contains intervals of major second, major third, augmented fourth, major sixth, and major seventh\n\n\nProvide the correct letter choice in \\boxed{X}, where X is the correct letter choice. Keep the explanation or feedback within 3 sentences.",
    "prediction": "qwen/qwen3.5-9b",
    "generated_result": {
      "generated_answer": "The whole-tone scale is a hexatonic scale consisting entirely of whole steps (major seconds). In 12-tone equal temperament, there are only two distinct whole-tone scales. The pitch class set for one is $\\{0, 2, 4, 6, 8, 10\\}$ and the other is $\\{1, 3, 5, 7, 9, 11\\}$ (where 0 = C). The \"B flat\" pitch class is 10. The pitch class E is 4. If a whole-tone scale contains pitch class 10 (B flat), it must belong to the set $\\{0, 2, 4, 6, 8, 10\\}$ (or its transposition), which necessarily includes pitch class 4 (E). Thus, statement C is correct.\n\nStatement D implies that the scale contains Major 7th (11 semitones) and Major 6th (9 semitones) intervals from the root. However, the intervals generated within the whole-tone scale relative to the root are Major 2nd (2 semitones), Major 3rd (4 semitones), Augmented 4th (6 semitones), Augmented 5th/Minor 6th (8 semitones), and Minor 7th/Major 6th enharmonic (10 semitones). Neither 9 nor 11 semitones are present in the set of differences of a whole-tone scale (which contains only even-numbered semitone intervals). Therefore, statement D is incorrect.\n\nBetween C (which is logically true based on pitch class sets) and D (which claims the existence of intervals not found in the scale's structure), D is the false statement.\n\n\\boxed{D}\n\nExplanation: The whole-tone scale is defined by intervals of major seconds (2 semitones), meaning all intervals between its notes are multiples of 2 semitones. This results in the presence of major seconds, major thirds, augmented fourths, and minor sevenths (or enharmonic equivalents), but it does not contain major sixths (9 semitones) or major sevenths (11 semitones), making statement D incorrect.\n\n\\boxed{D}",
      "success": true,
      "token_usage": {
        "input_tokens": 170,
        "output_tokens": 19446,
        "total_tokens": 19616
      },
      "provider": "openrouter",
      "error": null
    },
    "cost": 0.0029254,
