      "generated_answer": "\\boxed{B}\n\nThe text by William Bentinck focuses on moral \"horror\" towards \"innocent victims\" and the \"safety of the Empire,\" rather than economic gain or military conquest. This shifts the focus of imperialism toward humanitarian objectives, which challenges the commonly held assumption that Western imperialism was driven primarily or exclusively by economic profit motives (Option B).\n\nThe first passage actually supports Options A and C by demonstrating that British officials interfered in native religious practices to enforce British moral norms. Option D is less about armed force and more about \"power\" and prohibition. Thus, B is the only assumption undermined by the text's emphasis on conscience over profit.\n\n\\boxed{B}",
