      "generated_answer": "The question asks which of Robespierre's policies during the radical phase of the French Revolution was *least beneficial* to the defense of France. While many of his measures, such as conscripting large armies (J) or promoting soldiers by merit (G), directly strengthened military effectiveness, others like the de-Christianization campaign (D) alienated much of the peasant population and fueled internal dissent, such as the Vendée uprising, harming national unity and stability during a time of war.\n\nThus, pursuing a policy of de-Christianization was counterproductive to domestic cohesion and, by extension, national defense.\n\n\\boxed{D}",
